<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edigu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1352">1352</a>–<a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Edigey, Yedigey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edigu was an emir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghud">Mangit tribe</a> who, though not a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from about 1396 to 1411 through puppet khans. He defeated Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the former khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a> in 1399 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1408. His descendants founded the Nogai Horde, and he became a hero of Turkic epic poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edigu.jpg" alt="Edigu" width="320" height="589" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.12,_p.177_-_Edigu%27s_invasion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edigu, also written Edigü or Edigey, was a Turco-Mongol emir from the Manghit tribe. His father, Kutlukiya, was a noble who was defeated and killed by Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1378. Edigu became known as a highly successful general of Tokhtamysh before turning his arms against his master, and by 1396 he was a sovereign ruler of a large area between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and Ural rivers. According to the Epic of Edigu his paternal lineage goes back to the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Abu Bakr, but the historian István Vásáry considers this lineage, with its fifteen generations, to have been falsified for political legitimacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1397 Edigu allied himself with Timur-Qutlugh and was appointed commander-in-chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> armies. In 1399 he inflicted a crushing defeat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> and Vytautas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a>. Tokhtamysh lost the throne and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and Edigu united all of Jochi's lands under his rule, for the last time in history. He also took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, which he ruled until 1412, and deposed Timur-Qutlugh in favour of a more loyal khan. In 1406 his agents found and killed Tokhtamysh in Siberia.</p><p>In 1408 Edigu staged a destructive invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> because tribute had not been paid in several decades. He burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a> and other towns and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, but accepted a ransom from its inhabitants and returned to the steppe. By 1412 or 1413 he had lost control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>, and the Timurid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a> expelled him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>. Within a few years he held only the Manghit homeland on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural River</a> and the north-eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. He still ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> under Lithuanian rule in 1416.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Edigu was killed in 1419 by Qadir Berdi, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, who led a campaign deep into Manghit territory and died of his wounds shortly afterwards. Edigu came to be regarded as the founder of the Manghit ulus, later the Nogai Horde, whose dynasty continued for about two centuries, until his last descendants moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where they were baptized and became known as Princes Urusov and Yusupov. The Mamluk-era historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> described him as courageous and generous, a righteous Muslim who loved Islamic scholars, and said he prohibited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> from selling their sons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edigu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even so, the emir <b>Edigu</b> crushed Vytautas of Lithuania at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399 and besieged Moscow in 1408.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Vytautas suffered a heavy defeat against the Tatar commander <b>Edigu</b> at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399, but in 1410 his troops fought beside the Poles of Władysław II Jagiełło at the Battle of Grunwald, breaking the power of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 September 1380 Dmitry led a coalition of Rus' princes to victory over the Tatar commander Mamai at the Battle of Kulikovo on the upper Don, but two years later Khan Tokhtamysh took and burned Moscow, and in 1408 the emir <b>Edigu</b> besieged it again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nogai Horde grew from the Mangit tribe led by <b>Edigu</b>, the emir who dominated the Golden Horde around 1400.</blockquote><a href="/state/nogai-horde">Nogai Horde</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Golden Horde. Died in Saray-Jük, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.5,51.73&amp;year=1395" aria-label="Saray-Jük on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5759 498 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Edigu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6209 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6209 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.5,51.73&amp;year=1395">What was here in 1395? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
